ike enlarged hearts changes  to the yolk sac of the embryo the yolk  sac contains nutrients to allows an  embryo to grow twisted spines  compare this normal embryo to one where  the drug has been administered in early  stages of development a shortened tail  diminished yolk sac no eyes the earlier  the drugs given the more extensive the  damage and we also have fin defects so  you see the fins here away from the body  and you can see here that they're  damaged evolutionary the fins of the  fish gave rise to the limbs in us many  have waited decades for answers about  this drug and the peer review on the  aberdeen fish study is due in a matter  of days so it's odd that it's been  excluded especially considering today's  report uses 44 other non peer-reviewed  studies many of them were produced by  the manufacturer  makers of Prim das sheering now owned by  Bayer has released to the MHRA some of  the animal studies conducted just after  the drug was taken off the market in  1979 the compound was given to mice and  examination of embryos reported a  significant increase in visceral  malformations including the heart lung  and thorax wall at a high dose on this  the authors conclude the increase in  malformations in this study should be  considered drug-related I think some of  the animal studies that they refer to in  their report suggests that there is a  link clearly we need to do more work I  mean would you would you recommend the  use of this is a hormone pregnancy test  today you wouldn't no I wouldn't some of  the other animal studies don't appear to  show a link and regulators conclude that  overall they use outdated methods and  are inconclusive  I mean I think it's indisputable that  there's an association I might also say  MHRA and their predecessor organization  where the regulators and so I think  they're being defensive I'm not sure  they were the right people to put a  charge of a piece of work like this so  what does this report tell us about  regulatory failings the report authors  have explicitly decided not to examine  how the drug was regulated earlier this  year Sky News travelled to Berlin in  national archives we uncovered documents  showing whirring behavior by UK  regulators one of them warmed the  manufacturers that women who use prima  DOS had a five to one risk of giving  birth to a child with a malformation  but documents suggests the man working  for the Committee on safety of medicines  destroyed all the material on which his  investigation was based and he'd done  that to prevent individual claims being  based on his material it's something out  of spy story almost isn't it it's it's  the one can't really quite believe the  level of collusion and abuse of trust I  think many experts we've spoken to say  regulators should have been the key  focus of today's report ultimately what  we want to know is did the regulator do  a good job in looking after people at  the time and I don't know exactly how  that question might be asked I'd like it  if that question are answered in this  it's explicitly excluded in this report  shouldn't you be looking at the way it  was regulated our group was a group of  scientists and clinicians who have  particular expertise to look at the  evidence we were particularly not asked  to do and any review of the regulatory  issues around the time medicines has  moved on hugely in regulation and  medicines particularly we're now in a  very different environment that was not  our arena so nor can you say whether  there were any regulatory failings  we did not consider that we're a group  of scientists and experts who looked at  the signs you asked to draw lessons of  safety issues and communication isn't  that regulation this is a very  forward-looking report and we have made  at least 13 recommendations which we  have tasked the MHRA  our drug regulators to take forward but  campaigners say past mistakes should be  exposed people in authority did know  that there was a connectio